================================================================================
Worldwide Protein Data Bank (wwPDB) / RCSB PDB
================================================================================

The residue strings of the designed peptide parts -- epitope tags, affinity
tags, protease sites and linkers -- are verified against, and read out of,
deposited polymer entities of the PDB archive, retrieved through RCSB's data
API:
    https://data.rcsb.org/rest/v1/core/polymer_entity/

PDB archive data are dedicated to the public domain under the Creative Commons
Zero (CC0 1.0) waiver, stated in the wwPDB Usage Policy:
    https://www.wwpdb.org/about/usage-policies

    "Data files contained in the PDB archive are available under the CC0 1.0
     Universal (CC0 1.0) Public Domain Dedication."

That page is archived, with its sha256 and retrieval date, at
`legal/wwpdb-usage-policies.html`.

CC0 imposes no attribution requirement, and wwPDB *encourages* rather than
requires crediting the original depositors. The credit is given anyway, for the
same reason the Rfam credit below is: a database that trades on provenance
should say where its rows came from whether or not it is compelled to. Each
peptide row's `provenance.tsv` entry names the exact polymer entity its residues
were located in, and the row's `notes` names the residue offset at which it was
found.

ONE DISTINCTION MATTERS AND IS OBSERVED IN CODE. The CC0 dedication covers the
PDB *archive*; RCSB's own website content and annotation layer are separately
licensed under CC BY 4.0 (https://www.rcsb.org/pages/policies). This build reads
`entity_poly.pdbx_seq_one_letter_code_can` -- the deposited one-letter sequence
-- and nothing else. The deposited entity description is printed in the build
report so a reviewer can confirm the entity is the one named, and is
deliberately not written into any row.

