TWO EXCLUSIONS ARE ENFORCED AT INGEST, not merely intended:

  * miRBase-derived families are excluded. miRBase carries no licence at all and
    is 37.8% of Rfam (1,598 of 4,227 families). The exclusion keys on Rfam's
    `type` field and never on the family name: matching names beginning "mir"
    lets eight families through.
  * Wikipedia-derived prose is excluded. Every Rfam family carries a Wikipedia
    article pointer (`#=GF WK`) and most carry free-text comments (`#=GF CC`)
    that reproduce it, including inside `Rfam.seed.gz`. The parser keeps a
    named allow-list of Stockholm tags and drops `CC`, `WK` and `DR`
    unconditionally, so no Wikipedia text and no Sequence Ontology identifier
    enters this dataset. Our descriptions are written from the cited papers.

================================================================================
INSDC submitters
================================================================================

Sequence records reached through NCBI, ENA or DDBJ originate with the scientists
who deposited them. INSDC places no use restrictions or licensing requirements
on the records:
    https://www.insdc.org/policy/

Credit to the original submission is nevertheless expected, and the specific
accession behind each sequence is recorded in `provenance.tsv` and in each
record's `boundary_evidence` field.

================================================================================
Sources not used, listed so their absence is deliberate and legible
================================================================================

The following are NOT used, and their absence is a decision rather than an
oversight -- see `SOURCING.md` for the evidence behind each:

  * SnapGene Common Features, and pLannotate's snapgene.csv derived from it
  * Addgene bulk records
  * PlasMapper 3.0 FeatureDB (its own scrapeFeatures.py shows it is
    Addgene-scraped)
  * SEVA
  * UniVec, as a coordinate source
  * FPbase sequence data (its name-to-accession index only, pending resolution)
  * Sequence Ontology terms (pending resolution of a CC BY / CC BY-SA conflict
    between its LICENSE file and its README)
  * The Barrick et al. S1 variant sequences (extracted from Addgene plasmids;
    Addgene's terms permit noncommercial informational use only)

GenoLIB is deliberately absent from that list, because it was never assessed:
`SOURCING.md` contains no evidence about it either way, and listing it among
decisions would have implied one was made.

A CI taint gate transiently fetches pLannotate's `snapgene.csv` at a pinned
immutable commit, compares our description prose against it, and deletes it. No
byte of that file is committed or redistributed: the filename is in
`.gitignore`, and `tools/hooks/pre-commit` refuses any staged blob whose sha256
matches the pinned one, so renaming it does not get it in. It is implemented in
`features/build/taint_gate.py`, it runs in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, and it
fails CLOSED -- a network error exits with the status `taint-gate-unavailable`
rather than passing.

Its result at the time of writing, over all descriptions against all 1,367
of theirs: no row shares a contiguous five-token run, and no row reaches 60%
containment. Two rows exceed the 30% warning threshold, PLF:0002 and PLF:0003,
whose longest shared runs are one and two tokens; they are the two
aminoglycoside phosphotransferases, and what they share is the vocabulary of
the subject (kanamycin, neomycin, phosphotransferase, resistance), not phrasing.

Disclosing the gate is the point: it is the concrete evidence behind this
project's premise.

================================================================================
Changes made
================================================================================

CC BY 4.0 requires that changes be indicated. This dataset transforms its
sources rather than reproducing them:

  * Sequences are extracted from larger records and paired
    nucleotide-to-protein.
  * Every coding sequence is verified by translation against its reference
    protein; entries that do not translate exactly are dropped, not corrected.
  * Feature boundaries are computed from reading frames where they can be, and
    otherwise cite the publication that stipulated them.
  * Rfam seed rows are alignment rows, not sequences: they are degapped, and
    RNA is transformed to DNA (U -> T) unconditionally.
  * Designed peptide parts carry a residue string and no nucleotides: the
    peptide is located, exactly once, in a deposited PDB polymer entity fetched
    at build time. Back-translating a peptide is refused outright, because
    choosing codons would be writing a sequence no record contains.
  * All names, aliases and descriptions are written by the Polylinker
    contributors, and are marked `own-work` per field in `provenance.tsv`.

Provenance is recorded per FIELD, so the nucleotide reference, the protein
reference and our own editorial each cite the file they actually came from,
with that file's sha256 and retrieval date. Verifying a hash and then looking
for the data in the named file is expected to succeed; if it does not, that is
a bug in this dataset, not in your procedure.
